# Calibration set. Copy this file to seq.txt and replace the entries with your own.
#
#   cp seq.txt.example seq.txt
#
# One sequence per line, one-letter residue codes, upper or lower case. Blank lines and
# anything after a # are ignored. run.py reads this file when STATE = 1.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
#
# These peptides are not candidates and they are not seeds. They are the measurement that
# decides whether the rest of the pipeline means anything: peptides whose behaviour is already
# known experimentally, run through exactly the same modules, membranes and sampling as the
# campaign. If the observables do not separate the known classes, no optimiser can recover a
# signal that is not there, and the answer is to revisit the observable, the membranes or the
# imposed-helix assumption rather than to start a campaign.
#
# Aim for four classes, several peptides each, so that a class boundary is a boundary and not
# one point:
#
#   inactive              no activity against the target at reachable concentrations
#   selective             active on the target, little haemolysis
#   lytic                 active on both target and host (melittin-like)
#   binds without lysing  partitions into the membrane without permeabilising it
#
# The last class is the one that is usually left out and the one that matters most here. Both
# objectives are membrane-interaction observables, so a peptide that binds strongly and does
# nothing is exactly what a binding score can mistake for a hit. Without such peptides in the
# set, the calibration cannot show whether the observable distinguishes them.
#
# CHOOSING THEM
#
# Take the sequences from the primary literature or from a curated database (DRAMP, APD3,
# DBAASP), not from memory and not from this file, and record the MIC and haemolysis values and
# their source alongside your own copy. Keep the length range close to the campaign's
# (user/usersettings.py: min_length, max_length), because a 13-mer and a 37-mer are not
# comparable under a fixed box and a fixed imposed helix.
#
# A note on what the force field can represent: the all-H secondary structure handed to
# martinize2 means every peptide here is simulated as a helix whether or not it is one.
# Disulfide-stabilised and beta-sheet peptides (defensins, protegrins) will be misrepresented,
# and a proline-rich peptide bent in reality will be built straight. Including them is useful
# only as a deliberate test of that assumption, not as part of the ranking.

# Example of the format. Magainin 2, a selective alpha-helical AMP, is left here as a syntax
# example only: one peptide is not a calibration set, and this line should be replaced.
GIGKFLHSAKKFGKAFVGEIMNS

# inactive
# <sequence>   # name, MIC, source

# selective
# <sequence>   # name, MIC, haemolysis, source

# lytic
# <sequence>   # name, MIC, haemolysis, source

# binds without lysing
# <sequence>   # name, evidence of partitioning without permeabilisation, source
